@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "ASP.NET MVC";
    ViewData["ActiveMenu"] = "DotNetCore";
    ViewData["ActivePage"] = "Mvc";
}

<div class="container py-4">
    <p class="text-center lead">
        ASP.NET MVC (Model-View-Controller) is a structured and scalable framework for building web applications. It separates the application into distinct components, promoting clean architecture and maintainability.
    </p>
    <hr />

    <!-- Section: MVC Overview -->
    <div class="mt-5">
        <h2>What is the MVC Pattern?</h2>
        <p>
            MVC stands for:
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Model:</strong> Represents the data and business logic</li>
            <li><strong>View:</strong> Handles UI and rendering</li>
            <li><strong>Controller:</strong> Processes user input and coordinates the model and view</li>
        </ul>

        <p>
            This separation of concerns helps keep code organized, especially as applications grow.
        </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Section: Project Setup -->
    <div class="mt-5">
        <h3>Creating an ASP.NET MVC App</h3>
        <p>Use the Visual Studio template:</p>
        <ol>
            <li>Open Visual Studio → New Project → ASP.NET Core Web App (Model-View-Controller)</li>
            <li>Choose target framework (.NET 6 or later)</li>
            <li>Click Create</li>
        </ol>

        <p>The project will have folders for:</p>
        <ul>
            <li><code>Controllers/</code> – C# classes to handle requests</li>
            <li><code>Views/</code> – Razor templates for UI</li>
            <li><code>Models/</code> – Classes for data and logic</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <!-- Section: Example Controller -->
    <div class="mt-5">
        <h3>Example: Controller</h3>
        <div class="code-snippet bg-light p-3 rounded position-relative">
            <pre class="mb-0"><code>using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc;

public class HomeController : Controller
{
    public IActionResult Index()
    {
        return View(); // Returns Views/Home/Index.cshtml
    }
}</code></pre>
            <button class="btn btn-outline-secondary copy-btn position-absolute top-0 end-0 m-2">Copy</button>
        </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Section: Razor Views -->
    <div class="mt-5">
        <h3>Example: View (Razor)</h3>
        <p>Create <code>Views/Home/Index.cshtml</code>:</p>
        <div class="code-snippet bg-light p-3 rounded position-relative">
            <pre class="mb-0"><code>&lt;h1&gt;Welcome to MVC&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The current time is @DateTime.Now&lt;/p&gt;</code></pre>
            <button class="btn btn-outline-secondary copy-btn position-absolute top-0 end-0 m-2">Copy</button>
        </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Section: Passing Data to Views -->
    <div class="mt-5">
        <h3>Passing Data from Controller to View</h3>
        <p>Use ViewBag, ViewData, or strongly-typed models:</p>
        <div class="code-snippet bg-light p-3 rounded position-relative">
            <pre class="mb-0"><code>// Controller
public IActionResult About()
{
    ViewBag.Message = "This is a sample message.";
    return View();
}

// View
&lt;p&gt;@ViewBag.Message&lt;/p&gt;</code></pre>
            <button class="btn btn-outline-secondary copy-btn position-absolute top-0 end-0 m-2">Copy</button>
        </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Section: Forms and Routing -->
    <div class="mt-5">
        <h3>Handling Forms and Routing</h3>
        <p>Use model binding to handle form data and route it to controller actions:</p>
        <div class="code-snippet bg-light p-3 rounded position-relative">
            <pre class="mb-0"><code>&lt;form asp-action="Submit"&gt;
    &lt;input name="username" /&gt;
    &lt;button&gt;Go&lt;/button&gt;
&lt;/form&gt;

// Controller
[HttpPost]
public IActionResult Submit(string username)
{
    return Content("Hello, " + username);
}</code></pre>
            <button class="btn btn-outline-secondary copy-btn position-absolute top-0 end-0 m-2">Copy</button>
        </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Summary -->
    <div class="mt-5">
        <p>
            ASP.NET MVC is ideal for structured, testable, and scalable web apps. Next, we’ll dive into Entity Framework Core to learn how to connect your application to a database.
        </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Navigation Buttons -->
    <div class="d-flex justify-content-between mt-4">
        <a href="@Url.Action("WebPages", "DotNet")" class="btn btn-outline-secondary">
            &larr; Previous: ASP.NET Web Pages
        </a>
        <a href="@Url.Action("EFIntro", "DotNet")" class="btn btn-outline-secondary">
            Next: Introduction to Entity Framework Core &rarr;
        </a>
    </div>
</div>

<script>
    document.querySelectorAll('.copy-btn').forEach(btn => {
        btn.addEventListener('click', function () {
            const codeText = this.parentElement.querySelector('code')?.innerText;
            if (codeText) {
                navigator.clipboard.writeText(codeText).then(() => {
                    alert('Code copied to clipboard!');
                });
            }
        });
    });
</script>
